=========================================================
CONST CORRECTNESS IN C++ (INTERVIEW NOTES)
=========================================================

=========================================================
1. What is const?
=========================================================

const means the object cannot be modified after initialization.

Example:

const int x = 10;

// x = 20;   // Error

Benefits
---------
• Prevents accidental modification
• Improves code readability
• Makes APIs safer
• Helps compiler optimization
• Enables const correctness throughout code

=========================================================
2. Pointer and const
=========================================================

A) Pointer to const

const int x = 10;

const int* ptr = &x;
// int const* ptr = &x;   // Same

*ptr = 20;      // Error
ptr++;          // Allowed

Meaning
-------
• Data is constant
• Pointer can change


---------------------------------------------------------

B) Const Pointer

int x = 10;

int* const ptr = &x;

*ptr = 20;      // Allowed
ptr++;          // Error

Meaning
-------
• Pointer cannot change
• Data can change


---------------------------------------------------------

C) Const Pointer to Const Data

const int x = 10;

const int* const ptr = &x;

*ptr = 20;      // Error
ptr++;          // Error

Meaning
-------
Neither pointer nor data can change.

=========================================================
3. Const References
=========================================================

void print(const string& s)
{
    cout << s;
}

Advantages
----------
• No copy
• Cannot modify argument
• Faster
• Accepts temporaries

Example

print("Hello");

Works because parameter is const reference.

=========================================================
4. Const Member Functions ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

class Employee
{
    string name;

public:

    string getName() const
    {
        return name;
    }
};

Meaning
-------

The object cannot be modified inside the function.

Internally,

this

becomes

const Employee* const this;

Therefore,

name = "ABC";      // Error

=========================================================
5. Why Getter Functions are const?
=========================================================

string getName() const;

Reasons
-------
• Reading should not modify object
• Can be called on const objects

Example

const Employee e;

e.getName();

Without const, this won't compile.

=========================================================
6. Const Objects
=========================================================

const Employee e;

Can call

✔ const member functions

Cannot call

✘ non-const member functions

=========================================================
7. Function Parameters
=========================================================

Bad

void print(string s);

Good

void print(const string& s);

Advantages
----------
• No copy
• Faster
• Read-only
• Preferred for large objects

For small types (int, double, char), pass-by-value is usually better.

=========================================================
8. Returning const Reference
=========================================================

class Test
{
    string name;

public:

    const string& getName() const
    {
        return name;
    }
};

Advantages
----------
• No copy
• Read-only access

=========================================================
9. Returning const Value
=========================================================

const int getValue();

Usually avoid.

For primitive types it provides almost no benefit.

=========================================================
10. const with auto
=========================================================

const int x = 10;

auto a = x;          // int

const auto b = x;    // const int

=========================================================
11. constexpr vs const
=========================================================

const
-----

Read-only variable.

const int x = 10;

constexpr
----------

Compile-time constant.

constexpr int square(int x)
{
    return x * x;
}

Key Difference
--------------

const      -> Read-only

constexpr  -> Compile-time constant

=========================================================
12. mutable ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

Allows modification inside const functions.

class Logger
{
    mutable int accessCount = 0;

public:

    void print() const
    {
        accessCount++;
    }
};

Common Uses
-----------
• Cache
• Statistics
• Mutex
• Lazy initialization

=========================================================
13. const_cast ⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

Removes constness.

const int x = 10;

int* p = const_cast<int*>(&x);

Important
---------

Safe only if original object was NOT declared const.

Example

void update(int* p)
{
    *p = 20;
}

void foo(const int& x)
{
    update(const_cast<int*>(&x));
}

Never modify an object originally declared const.

Doing so results in Undefined Behavior (UB).

=========================================================
14. Overloading const Member Functions ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

class Test
{
public:

    void show()
    {
        cout << "Non-Const";
    }

    void show() const
    {
        cout << "Const";
    }
};

Usage

Test t;
t.show();          // Non-const version

const Test t2;
t2.show();         // Const version

=========================================================
15. Const Iterator
=========================================================

vector<int> v{1,2,3};

vector<int>::const_iterator it = v.begin();

*it = 10;      // Error

++it;          // Allowed

Meaning
-------
Iterator moves
Element cannot change

=========================================================
16. Const Container
=========================================================

const vector<int> v{1,2,3};

v.push_back(4);      // Error

Container cannot be modified.

=========================================================
17. Top-Level vs Low-Level const ⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

Top-Level const

const int a = 10;

Object itself is const.

-----------------------------------------

Low-Level const

const int* p;

Pointed object is const.

Important for
-------------
• Templates
• auto
• Type deduction

=========================================================
18. const in Smart Pointers
=========================================================

const unique_ptr<int> p = make_unique<int>(10);

• Pointer cannot be reassigned.
• Managed object can still be modified.

-----------------------------------------

unique_ptr<const int> p = make_unique<const int>(10);

• Object cannot be modified.
• Pointer can be moved.

-----------------------------------------

const unique_ptr<const int> p = make_unique<const int>(10);

Neither pointer nor object can be modified.

=========================================================
19. Const in Range-Based For Loop
=========================================================

vector<string> names;

for(const auto& name : names)
{
    cout << name;
}

Preferred because

• No copies
• Read-only
• Efficient

=========================================================
20. Const and STL Algorithms
=========================================================

const vector<int> v{1,2,3};

for(auto x : v)
    cout << x;

Reading is allowed.

sort(v.begin(), v.end());     // Error

Cannot modify const container.

=========================================================
INTERVIEW QUESTIONS
=========================================================

Q1. Why use const correctness?

Answer
------
• Prevent accidental modification
• Better API design
• Improves readability
• Easier maintenance
• Helps optimization

---------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Difference between

const int* p;

and

int* const p;

Answer

const int* p

• Pointer can change
• Data cannot

int* const p

• Pointer cannot change
• Data can

---------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Why are getter functions const?

Answer

Because reading should not modify object state.

Also allows calling getters on const objects.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Can a const member function modify data?

Normally No.

Exceptions

• mutable members
• Objects pointed to by non-const pointers/references

---------------------------------------------------------

Q5. What is mutable?

Allows selected data members to change even inside const member functions.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Can constructors be const?

No.

Constructors initialize objects.

The concept of a const constructor does not exist.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Can static member functions be const?

No.

Static member functions have no 'this' pointer.

const applies only to member functions with a this pointer.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q8. What does const after a member function mean?

Example

void display() const;

Meaning

The function promises not to modify the object's observable state (except mutable members).

---------------------------------------------------------

Q9. What is the type of 'this' inside a const member function?

const ClassName* const this;

---------------------------------------------------------

Q10. When should we pass by const reference?

Use const T& when

• Object is large
• No modification required

Examples

const string&
const vector<int>&
const Employee&

Pass small types (int, double, char, bool) by value.

